        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Dome of the Rock</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 691 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Ordered by the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Abd al-Malik; supervised by Raja ibn Haywa and Yazid ibn Sallam with his son Baha, using expert workers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> who were presumably Christians</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">An octagonal shrine with a dome about 20 metres across on a drum carried by 16 supports, two ambulatories round the rock, four entrances, 36 windows and about 1,200 square metres of mosaic</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">At the centre of the Al-Aqsa mosque compound on the Temple Mount in the Old City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the earliest surviving major work of Islamic architecture; the Foundation Stone beneath its dome; the earliest inscriptions proclaiming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and Muhammad; Umayyad mosaics; Ottoman tilework; a gold-plated dome; its place in Muslim, Jewish and Christian belief</span></div>
