    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Abd al-Malik had the shrine built during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-fitna">Second Fitna</a>, and its inscription date, 72 AH (691/2), is the year most historians take as the completion of the original building.</li><li>Muslims, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Christians each attach significance to the rock beneath the dome: Muslims to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isra-and-miraj">Night Journey</a> of Muhammad, Jews to the Temple, and Christians and Jews to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a>'s attempted sacrifice of his son.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> made it a church, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> reconsecrated it, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> covered its walls in tile, and the Jordanian restoration of 1959 to 1964 gave it its gold-plated roof.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The dedicatory inscription inside the dome originally named Abd al-Malik, but his name was later removed and replaced by that of the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>, while the date 72 AH was left unchanged.</p></aside>
    </section>

