    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">691</span><span class="rf-text">The original dome was completed in 72 AH (691/2), the year most historians accept, on the orders of Abd al-Malik</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1015</span><span class="rf-text">The upper part of the dome collapsed in an earthquake and was rebuilt in 1022 and 1023 under the Fatimid al-Zahir</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1099</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and gave the shrine to the Augustinians, who turned it into a church</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1187</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, reconsecrated the shrine and replaced the cross on the dome with a crescent</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1545</span><span class="rf-text">Ottoman tiles dated to this year appear on the exterior, part of the renovation begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1927</span><span class="rf-text">Parts of the building collapsed in an earthquake on 11 July and the walls were left badly cracked</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1959</span><span class="rf-text">Jordan began a restoration that lasted until 1964 and replaced the lead on the dome with gold-plated aluminium-bronze</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1993</span><span class="rf-text">The gold covering of the dome was refurbished with funds from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-of-jordan">King Hussein</a> of Jordan</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre.jpg" alt="Church of the Holy Sepulchre" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Church_of_the_Holy_Sepulchre_by_Gerd_Eichmann_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gerd Eichmann, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Dome of the Rock stands at the centre of the Temple Mount, the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s Temple and of the Second Jewish Temple, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> greatly expanded in the 1st century BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> destroyed Herod's Temple in 70 CE, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Bar Kokhba</a> revolt in 135 CE the emperor Hadrian built a Roman temple to Jupiter Capitolinus there. Under Byzantine rule from the 4th to the 6th centuries Christian pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> developed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a> was built in the 320s, but the Temple Mount was left undeveloped after a failed project to restore the Jewish Temple under the emperor Julian.</p><p>In 638 the Arab armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-al-khattab">Umar ibn al-Khattab</a> conquered Byzantine Jerusalem. Umar was advised by Ka'b al-Ahbar, a rabbi who had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, that the site was identical with that of the former Jewish Temples. Among the first Muslims Jerusalem was called Madinat bayt al-Maqdis, the City of the Temple. The shrine was ordered by the Umayyad caliph Abd al-Malik, who reigned from 685 to 705, during the Second Fitna. Its octagonal plan may have been influenced by the Byzantine-era Church of the Seat of Mary, known as Kathisma in Greek, built between 451 and 458 on the road between Jerusalem and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Dome of the Rock&#8221; cites where it covers Pre-Islamic background, Umayyads, Abbasids and Fatimids, Ayyubids and Mamluks:</p><ul><li>Moshe Gil, A History of Palestine, 634–1099 (Cambridge University Press, 1997)</li><li>Amikam Elad, Medieval Jerusalem and Islamic Worship: Holy Places, Ceremonies, Pilgrimage (Brill, 1999)</li><li>Jennifer A. Pruitt, Building the Caliphate: Construction, Destruction, and Sectarian Identity in Early Fatimid Architecture (Yale University Press, 2020)</li><li>Marcus Milwright, The Dome of the Rock and its Umayyad Mosaic Inscriptions (Edinburgh University Press, 2016)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jesus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jesus.jpg" alt="Jesus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spas_vsederzhitel_sinay_(cropped1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The plan is essentially octagonal. A dome about 20 metres across, set on an elevated circular drum, stands on 16 supports, four piers and twelve columns. An octagonal arcade of 24 piers and columns surrounds this circle, and the two together form an inner ambulatory encircling the holy rock; the outer octagon forms a second ambulatory. Each outer wall measures about 60 metres wide and 36 metres high. There are four entrances, one for each cardinal direction, and the outer wall has 36 windows.</p><p>Mosaics cover about 1,200 square metres of the interior, with stylised plants and trees, geometric patterns and jewellery drawn from late antique Byzantine and Sasanian art. The columns and capitals were reused from older buildings. The Umayyad inscriptions on the octagonal arcades are in early Kufic script and mix Qur'anic passages with the Shahada. One frieze quotes verses from the Qur'an on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, son of Mary, including a rejection of the idea that God should take a child. Outside, the Ottoman tilework carries Surah Ya-Sin, with Surah 17, Al-Isra', which tells of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isra-and-miraj">Night Journey</a>, inscribed above it.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Dome of the Rock&#8221; cites where it covers Structure, Interior decoration, Exterior decoration, Homages in art and architecture:</p><ul><li>Marcus Milwright, The Dome of the Rock and its Umayyad Mosaic Inscriptions (Edinburgh University Press, 2016)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muawiya-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muawiya-i.jpg" alt="Muawiya I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muawiya-i">Muawiya I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arab-Sasanian_coin_of_Muawiyah_I,_struck_at_the_Fasa_mint_in_Darabjird_(Fars).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LouisAragon (uploader), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The structure was built by order of Abd al-Malik, and it had basically the same shape as it does today. The date of its start is given differently: Sibt ibn al-Jawzi says construction began in 685/6, and al-Suyuti puts its commencement in 688. A dedicatory inscription in Kufic script gives the year 72 AH (691/2), which most historians believe is the year the original Dome was completed; an alternative reading takes it to be the year construction started. Some scholars have suggested that the dome was added to an existing building, either one built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muawiya-i">Muawiyah</a> I or a Byzantine building from the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>.</p><p>The supervisor and engineer were Raja ibn Haywa, Yazid ibn Sallam and Yazid's son Baha. Abd al-Malik was represented in the supervision by his son Sa'id al-Khayr, and he brought expert workers from across his domain, then restricted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Construction cost was reportedly seven times the yearly tax income of Egypt. The plan and mosaics were patterned on nearby Byzantine churches and palaces; K. A. C. Creswell noted that the builders used the measurements of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr.jpg" alt="Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_dirham_of_Abd_Allah_ibn_al-Zubayr_690-91.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Not credited, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Medieval sources give varying motives for Abd al-Malik. One series of explanations holds that, at war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and with the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</a>, who controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, he wanted a monument of victory that set <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> apart in a city of older Abrahamic faiths. Shelomo Dov Goitein argued that the rotunda was meant to rival the many Christian domes, and others name the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a> in particular. A second account holds that he wished to divert Muslims from the Ka'aba, where Ibn al-Zubayr condemned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> at the pilgrimage. Most modern historians dismiss this as anti-Umayyad propaganda, though others concede that it cannot be conclusively dismissed.</p><p>Muslims hold that Muhammad's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isra-and-miraj">Night Journey</a> began from the rock. The early sources suggest this was not yet fully formulated when the Dome was built, and its inscriptions of 691/2 do not mention it. Ibn Ishaq, in the early 8th century, wrote that Muhammad ascended to heaven from the Rock. Some Islamic scholars say instead that he ascended from the Masjid Al-Aqsa, of which the Dome is a part. Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> believe the site is where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> attempted to sacrifice his son. Jewish tradition holds it to be where God created the world and Adam.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saladin.jpg" alt="Saladin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Nasir_I_Salah_al-Din_Yusuf_(Saladin)._AH_564-589_(1169-1193_CE)_%C3%86_Dirham_(30.1mm,_13.28_g,_6h)._Without_mint-name._Dated_AH_586_(AD_1190-91)._Sultan_sitting_facing,_cross-legged,_on_high-backed_throne_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group (CNG), CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Earthquakes damaged the building in 808 and 846, and the upper part of the dome fell in 1015; it was rebuilt in 1022 and 1023 under the Fatimid al-Zahir, who also repaired the drum's mosaics in 1027 and 1028. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1099 the Augustinians made the shrine a church. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a>, who regarded it as the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, called it the Templum Domini and put it on their Grand Masters' seals, and it became the model for round Templar churches across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> recaptured the city on 2 October 1187, had the Crusaders' marble cover removed from the rock, and reconsecrated the shrine.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultans restored the exterior and re-gilded the dome, which was severely damaged in 1447. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> replaced the old exterior mosaics with tiles, with inscriptions dated 1545 and 1552. A restoration of 1874 and 1875 replaced tiles on the west and southwest walls. The earthquake of 11 July 1927 caused parts to collapse. Between 1959 and 1964 Jordan led a restoration funded by Arab governments and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, replacing tiles with copies made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The 1993 refurbishment of the golden covering used 80 kilograms of gold funded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-of-jordan">King Hussein</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent.jpg" alt="Suleiman the Magnificent" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EmperorSuleiman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Circle of Titian, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> designated the Dome of the Rock a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, and its golden dome dominates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>'s skyline. According to the art historian Oleg Grabar it remains a unique monument of Islamic culture in almost all respects, a work of art and a cultural and pious document. Its Islamic inscriptions became a model, as afterwards such inscriptions became a common feature in Islamic structures and almost always mention Muhammad. Its architecture inspired later buildings, including the 15th-century octagonal Church of St. Giacomo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the Rumbach Street Synagogue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>, the mausoleum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and the New Synagogue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p><p>The compound is a subject of dispute today. The Dome is maintained by the Ministry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waqf">Awqaf</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Amman</a>. In 1967, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a>, an Israeli flag was raised over it and lowered a few hours later on the orders of Moshe Dayan, who gave the Muslim waqf authority to manage the Al-Aqsa. Under the arrangement known as the Status Quo, non-Muslims may visit at set hours but may not pray there; the Chief Rabbinate of Israel maintains a ban on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> entering any part of the site.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Dome of the Rock is an octagonal shrine on the Temple Mount in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, ordered by the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Abd al-Malik and completed in 691/2. It has been a church, a Muslim shrine again and a monument of the tilework and gold of later rulers, and it holds meaning for Muslims, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Christians.</em></p>

